    // distinguishes the float 2.0 from the int 2, or a uint8 from an int. What a
    // written-out conversion does give us is the target type at EMIT time, and that is
    // enough for the two places where Go's answer differs from the untyped one:
    // division (float64(i)/2 is 2.5, not 2) and the complement or wrap of a sized
    // unsigned type (^uint8(0xF0) is 0x0F). The type cannot ride ON the thunk: a thunk
